{"module":"incarceration","voice":"male","duration":246.3,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Mass incarceration and the war on drugs","start":0.0,"end":3.36,"words":[[0.05,0.37],[0.37,1.64],[1.64,1.96],[1.96,2.17],[2.17,2.38],[2.38,2.59],[2.59,3.12]]},{"el":"intro","text":"A 911 call can be the first step into the criminal legal system.","start":4.16,"end":9.91,"words":[[4.21,4.36],[4.36,5.85],[5.85,6.08],[6.08,6.31],[6.31,6.47],[6.47,6.63],[6.63,6.94],[6.94,7.25],[7.25,7.56],[7.56,7.72],[7.72,8.34],[8.34,8.74],[8.74,9.28]]},{"el":"intro","text":"This module shows how big that system became, who it falls on, and the laws behind it.","start":10.26,"end":18.02,"words":[[10.3,10.6],[10.6,11.2],[11.2,11.59],[11.59,11.89],[11.89,12.19],[12.19,12.48],[12.48,13.08],[13.08,13.77],[14.12,14.32],[14.32,14.52],[14.52,14.91],[14.91,15.21],[15.56,15.85],[15.85,16.05],[16.05,16.35],[16.35,17.04],[17.04,17.34]]},{"el":"h0","text":"The numbers","start":18.37,"end":19.45,"words":[[18.41,18.62],[18.62,19.24]]},{"el":"s0","text":"About 1.9 million people are held in U.S. prisons, jails and detention centers (Prison Policy Initiative), up from about 300,000 in the early 1970s.","start":20.15,"end":32.46,"words":[[20.19,20.56],[20.56,21.46],[21.46,21.91],[21.91,22.29],[22.29,22.36],[22.36,22.66],[22.66,22.81],[22.81,23.19],[23.56,24.16],[24.42,24.87],[24.87,25.1],[25.1,25.7],[25.7,26.15],[26.15,26.59],[26.59,27.04],[27.04,27.79],[28.06,28.21],[28.21,28.51],[28.51,28.88],[28.88,30.15],[30.15,30.3],[30.3,30.45],[30.45,30.68],[30.68,31.88]]},{"el":"s0","text":"No country locks up a larger share of its people.","start":32.81,"end":35.82,"words":[[32.85,33.05],[33.05,33.46],[33.46,33.73],[33.73,33.87],[33.87,34.0],[34.0,34.34],[34.34,34.55],[34.55,34.69],[34.69,34.89],[34.89,35.3]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Black Americans are imprisoned in state prisons at nearly five times the rate of white Americans (Sentencing Project, 2021).","start":36.17,"end":44.82,"words":[[36.21,36.5],[36.5,37.16],[37.16,37.23],[37.23,37.88],[37.88,38.03],[38.03,38.39],[38.39,38.89],[38.89,39.04],[39.04,39.4],[39.4,39.69],[39.69,40.05],[40.05,40.2],[40.2,40.49],[40.49,40.63],[40.63,40.92],[40.92,41.58],[41.58,42.23],[42.23,42.88],[43.13,44.29]]},{"el":"s0","text":"In 2010, Pew found that about 1 in 9 Black children had a parent behind bars.","start":45.17,"end":52.09,"words":[[45.22,45.39],[45.39,46.23],[46.52,46.77],[46.77,47.19],[47.19,47.44],[47.44,47.86],[47.86,48.11],[48.11,48.28],[48.28,48.62],[48.62,48.95],[48.95,49.62],[49.62,49.87],[49.87,50.04],[50.04,50.63],[50.63,51.21],[51.21,51.55]]},{"el":"h1","text":"“Except as a punishment for crime”","start":52.99,"end":55.57,"words":[[53.04,53.58],[53.58,53.76],[53.76,53.94],[53.94,54.76],[54.76,54.94],[54.94,55.39]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The 13th Amendment's exception, which made convict leasing legal after slavery (see the module on policing), still allows forced labor in prisons.","start":56.27,"end":65.62,"words":[[56.32,56.47],[56.47,56.91],[56.91,57.64],[57.64,58.31],[58.56,58.86],[58.86,59.15],[59.15,59.67],[59.67,60.03],[60.03,60.4],[60.4,60.7],[60.7,61.28],[61.28,61.43],[61.43,61.58],[61.58,62.02],[62.02,62.17],[62.17,62.76],[63.01,63.31],[63.31,63.67],[63.67,63.97],[63.97,64.34],[64.34,64.48],[64.48,65.07]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Starting with Colorado in 2018, several states have removed similar exceptions from their own constitutions; others have voted to keep them.","start":65.97,"end":75.52,"words":[[66.01,66.45],[66.45,66.67],[66.67,67.33],[67.33,67.48],[67.48,68.44],[68.7,69.14],[69.14,69.58],[69.58,69.81],[69.81,70.25],[70.25,70.69],[70.69,71.35],[71.35,71.65],[71.65,71.87],[71.87,72.09],[72.09,73.13],[73.38,73.68],[73.68,73.9],[73.9,74.34],[74.34,74.49],[74.49,74.71],[74.71,75.01]]},{"el":"h2","text":"The war on drugs","start":76.42,"end":77.94,"words":[[76.47,76.7],[76.7,76.94],[76.94,77.17],[77.17,77.75]]},{"el":"s2","text":"President Nixon called drug abuse “public enemy number one” in 1971.","start":78.64,"end":84.4,"words":[[78.67,79.37],[79.37,79.84],[79.84,80.15],[80.15,80.46],[80.46,80.85],[80.85,81.32],[81.32,81.7],[81.7,82.09],[82.09,82.33],[82.33,82.48],[82.48,83.88]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The Anti-Drug Abuse Act of 1986 set the same mandatory minimum sentence for 5 grams of crack as for 500 grams of powder cocaine, a 100-to-1 ratio that fell overwhelmingly on Black defendants.","start":84.75,"end":102.68,"words":[[84.79,84.98],[84.98,85.73],[85.73,86.21],[86.21,86.49],[86.49,86.68],[86.68,88.09],[88.09,88.37],[88.37,88.56],[88.56,88.94],[88.94,89.79],[89.79,90.45],[90.45,91.11],[91.11,91.3],[91.3,91.67],[91.67,92.15],[92.15,92.33],[92.33,92.71],[92.71,92.9],[92.9,93.09],[93.09,94.13],[94.13,94.6],[94.6,94.79],[94.79,95.26],[95.26,96.01],[96.34,96.53],[96.53,97.94],[97.94,98.7],[98.7,98.98],[98.98,99.26],[99.26,100.4],[100.4,100.58],[100.58,100.96],[100.96,102.0]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Congress cut the ratio to 18-to-1 in 2010 (Fair Sentencing Act) and made that retroactive in 2018 (First Step Act).","start":103.03,"end":111.85,"words":[[103.08,103.61],[103.61,103.84],[103.84,103.99],[103.99,104.6],[104.6,104.75],[104.75,105.52],[105.52,105.67],[105.67,106.35],[106.35,106.58],[106.58,107.27],[107.27,107.5],[107.76,107.99],[107.99,108.3],[108.3,108.53],[108.53,109.36],[109.36,109.52],[109.52,110.43],[110.43,110.74],[110.74,111.04],[111.04,111.35]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Black and white Americans use marijuana at similar rates, but in 2018 Black people were 3.6 times more likely to be arrested for possessing it (ACLU, 2020).","start":112.2,"end":124.77,"words":[[112.24,112.57],[112.57,112.82],[112.82,113.15],[113.15,113.89],[113.89,114.14],[114.14,114.8],[114.8,114.96],[114.96,115.46],[115.46,115.95],[116.24,116.49],[116.49,116.65],[116.65,117.64],[117.64,117.97],[117.97,118.39],[118.39,118.55],[118.55,119.54],[119.54,119.95],[119.95,120.12],[120.12,120.61],[120.61,120.78],[120.78,120.94],[120.94,121.52],[121.52,121.68],[121.68,122.26],[122.26,122.43],[122.43,122.84],[123.13,124.2]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Did the war on drugs aim at Black communities?","start":125.67,"end":128.99,"words":[[125.72,125.96],[125.96,126.12],[126.12,126.28],[126.28,126.43],[126.43,126.83],[126.83,127.06],[127.06,127.22],[127.22,127.54],[127.54,128.4]]},{"el":"s3","text":"In a 2016 Harper's article, writer Dan Baum quoted Nixon aide John Ehrlichman as saying, in a 1994 interview, that the administration linked Black people with heroin in order to disrupt their communities.","start":129.69,"end":144.78,"words":[[129.73,129.89],[129.89,130.04],[130.04,131.05],[131.05,131.43],[131.43,131.97],[132.24,132.63],[132.63,132.86],[132.86,133.17],[133.17,133.71],[133.71,134.17],[134.17,134.4],[134.4,134.71],[134.71,135.48],[135.48,135.64],[135.64,136.1],[136.37,136.53],[136.53,136.68],[136.68,137.84],[137.84,138.46],[138.73,138.96],[138.96,139.11],[139.11,140.19],[140.19,140.58],[140.58,140.89],[140.89,141.27],[141.27,141.5],[141.5,142.04],[142.04,142.2],[142.2,142.43],[142.43,142.58],[142.58,143.12],[143.12,143.36],[143.36,144.2]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Ehrlichman's family disputes the quote, and he can't confirm it (he died in 1999).","start":145.13,"end":151.43,"words":[[145.17,145.96],[145.96,146.39],[146.39,146.96],[146.96,147.1],[147.1,147.53],[147.79,148.0],[148.0,148.15],[148.15,148.43],[148.43,148.86],[148.86,149.01],[149.01,149.15],[149.15,149.44],[149.44,149.58],[149.58,150.87]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Historians agree the drug war's effects fell hardest on Black communities.","start":151.78,"end":156.93,"words":[[151.82,152.64],[152.64,152.97],[152.97,153.14],[153.14,153.47],[153.47,153.71],[153.71,154.21],[154.21,154.45],[154.45,154.95],[154.95,155.11],[155.11,155.44],[155.44,156.35]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Whether that was the goal from the start is debated.","start":157.28,"end":160.21,"words":[[157.34,157.6],[157.6,157.79],[157.79,157.98],[157.98,158.11],[158.11,158.36],[158.36,158.62],[158.62,158.75],[158.75,159.0],[159.0,159.13],[159.13,159.7]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The 1994 Crime Bill is debated too.","start":160.56,"end":164.35,"words":[[160.6,160.77],[160.77,162.04],[162.04,162.47],[162.47,162.72],[162.72,162.89],[162.89,163.57],[163.57,163.83]]},{"el":"s3","text":"It funded police and prisons and encouraged “three strikes” and truth-in-sentencing laws.","start":164.7,"end":170.15,"words":[[164.75,164.89],[164.89,165.32],[165.32,165.69],[165.69,165.9],[165.9,166.41],[166.41,166.63],[166.63,167.28],[167.28,167.49],[167.49,168.0],[168.0,168.22],[168.22,169.3],[169.3,169.59]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Some Black leaders and mayors supported it at the time because of real violence in their communities.","start":170.5,"end":176.62,"words":[[170.54,170.77],[170.77,171.08],[171.08,171.47],[171.47,171.7],[171.7,172.01],[172.01,172.55],[172.55,172.7],[172.7,172.85],[172.85,173.01],[173.01,173.32],[173.32,173.7],[173.7,173.86],[173.86,174.16],[174.16,174.78],[174.78,174.93],[174.93,175.17],[175.17,176.01]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Researchers still argue over how much it drove incarceration, since most prisoners are held under state laws.","start":176.97,"end":185.43,"words":[[177.02,177.75],[177.75,178.11],[178.11,178.47],[178.47,178.83],[178.83,179.1],[179.1,179.47],[179.47,179.65],[179.65,180.1],[180.1,181.28],[181.6,181.96],[181.96,182.41],[182.41,183.14],[183.14,183.23],[183.23,183.59],[183.59,183.95],[183.95,184.41],[184.41,184.77]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Punishment after the sentence","start":186.33,"end":188.34,"words":[[186.38,187.1],[187.1,187.42],[187.42,187.58],[187.58,188.14]]},{"el":"s4","text":"A record can follow someone for life: it can bar them from jobs, housing, student aid and, in many states, voting (see the module on the vote).","start":189.04,"end":198.34,"words":[[189.09,189.23],[189.23,189.58],[189.58,189.79],[189.79,190.14],[190.14,190.57],[190.57,190.71],[190.71,191.06],[191.3,191.44],[191.44,191.65],[191.65,191.79],[191.79,192.0],[192.0,192.28],[192.28,192.7],[192.95,193.44],[193.68,194.25],[194.25,194.46],[194.46,194.74],[194.98,195.12],[195.12,195.4],[195.4,195.89],[196.14,196.56],[196.56,196.7],[196.7,196.84],[196.84,197.26],[197.26,197.4],[197.4,197.54],[197.54,197.89]]},{"el":"s4","text":"“Ban the box” rules, first adopted by Hawaii in 1998, now delay criminal-history questions in many public and private hiring processes.","start":198.69,"end":208.71,"words":[[198.74,198.96],[198.96,199.1],[199.1,199.38],[199.38,199.74],[199.99,200.27],[200.27,200.77],[200.77,200.98],[200.98,201.41],[201.41,201.55],[201.55,202.83],[203.08,203.3],[203.3,203.65],[203.65,204.72],[204.72,205.36],[205.36,205.5],[205.5,205.79],[205.79,206.21],[206.21,206.43],[206.43,206.92],[206.92,207.42],[207.42,208.13]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Since Pennsylvania in 2018, a growing number of states have passed “Clean Slate” laws that automatically seal eligible old records.","start":209.06,"end":218.88,"words":[[209.11,209.44],[209.44,210.44],[210.44,210.61],[210.61,211.69],[211.98,212.15],[212.15,212.65],[212.65,213.06],[213.06,213.23],[213.23,213.73],[213.73,213.98],[213.98,214.31],[214.31,214.64],[214.64,215.06],[215.06,215.31],[215.31,215.56],[215.56,216.39],[216.39,216.64],[216.64,217.39],[217.39,217.72],[217.72,218.3]]},{"el":"h5","text":"What helps","start":219.78,"end":220.68,"words":[[219.82,220.05],[220.05,220.43]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Expungement clinics, Clean Slate laws, reentry programs, and alternatives to police for crisis calls (see the module on policing) all reduce the harm.","start":221.38,"end":233.03,"words":[[221.43,222.43],[222.43,223.11],[223.4,223.74],[223.74,224.16],[224.16,224.49],[224.78,225.46],[225.46,226.3],[226.59,226.84],[226.84,227.68],[227.68,227.85],[227.85,228.27],[228.27,228.44],[228.44,229.02],[229.02,229.36],[229.36,229.53],[229.53,229.69],[229.69,230.2],[230.2,230.37],[230.37,230.95],[231.25,231.42],[231.42,231.92],[231.92,232.09],[232.09,232.42]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Lawyers and paralegals can make a direct difference: many people eligible to clear a record never do because they don't know how.","start":233.38,"end":241.82,"words":[[233.43,233.82],[233.82,234.05],[234.05,234.81],[234.81,235.04],[235.04,235.34],[235.34,235.5],[235.5,236.03],[236.03,236.64],[236.91,237.22],[237.22,237.6],[237.6,238.28],[238.28,238.44],[238.44,238.74],[238.74,238.9],[238.9,239.28],[239.28,239.58],[239.58,239.74],[239.74,240.12],[240.12,240.35],[240.35,240.73],[240.73,240.96],[240.96,241.26]]},{"el":"s5","text":"The quest for this module asks you to help someone do it.","start":242.17,"end":245.44,"words":[[242.21,242.35],[242.35,242.67],[242.67,242.8],[242.8,243.0],[243.0,243.39],[243.39,243.65],[243.65,243.78],[243.78,243.91],[243.91,244.17],[244.17,244.56],[244.56,244.7],[244.7,244.89]]}]}